= Fundamental Definitions for Matroids<chapter1>

The goal of matroid theory is to provide an abstract theory of
independence. Matroids have their roots in algerba --- specifically
_linear algebra_, graph theory, and combinatorics; and each field
provides a distinct flavor of the subject. The notion of
indepenedence was first covered by Whitney in 1935, and then Van der
Waerden in 1937 in his seminal work "Moderne Algebra". Gian Carlo
Rota would also stumble upon the theory independently. Pioneering
work would later be done by Tutte, Nakasawa, Birkhoff, and Mac Lane.

The most important aspect of matroids is that they have several
equivalent definitions. The purpose of chapter here is to introduce
matroids under these different definitions.

